22:25:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The fact that electrons flow from negative to positive doesn’t force us to ground the positive terminal—it’s a design choice. In cars, we use **negative ground** (the chassis is connected to the battery’s negative terminal) primarily for two reasons: ## 1. Corrosion prevention (the big one) When metal parts are exposed to moisture and road salt, galvanic corrosion can occur—especially where different metals meet. If the car body were connected to the positive terminal (positive ground), the chassis would act as an **anode** in any accidental electrolytic cell, causing metal ions to leave the body and accelerating rust. By grounding the negative terminal, the chassis becomes the **cathode**, which tends to receive electrons and resist corrosion. This switch (most cars changed from positive to negative ground in the 1950s–60s) dramatically reduced frame and body rot. ## 2. Compatibility with modern electronics Semiconductor circuits (especially those built around NPN transistors, the most common type in automotive electronics) are simpler and cheaper to design with a **common negative rail**. Negative ground became the natural standard once solid‑state electronics found their way into vehicles. ## History bonus: it wasn’t always this way Early automobiles (pre‑1950s) often used **positive ground**. The move to negative ground coincided with the wider adoption of 12 V systems and the corrosion‑fighting benefits described above. Today, practically all road vehicles use negative ground. So: electron flow direction (negative → positive) doesn’t dictate which side is “ground.” It’s the other way around—engineers pick the ground reference that best protects the vehicle and simplifies its electrical design.
